o three left. But I can't get it unless someone discards or I draw. But since I have to play a tile now, maybe keeping B9s for possible kong later.\n\nWait, the current hand has two B9s. If I play a B9, then I have one left. If I keep both, maybe I can form a pung if I draw another. But since the unshown B9 is 3, there are three left. So possible. But playing a B9 might be safe if others don't need it.\n\nLooking at the other tiles. T1, T8. Maybe forming a chow with T1 and T2, T3? But again, no T2 or T3 in hand. The T3 unshown is 4, so available. But I don't have it. So maybe not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, looking for all terminals and honors. Since I already have two pungs of J2 and F2, which are honors. If I can form all terminals and honors, that's a 32-point pattern. Let's see: All terminals and honors require only terminal (1 or 9 of suits) and honor tiles. My current hand has T1, T8, T9, B1, B3, B9. Wait, T8 is a middle tile (not terminal), so that's a problem. So if I discard T8, then my hand would be T1, T9, B1, B3, B9, and the two B9s. Wait, no, the current hand is T1, T8, T9x2, B1, B3, B9x2. If I discard T8, then the remaining tiles are T1, T9x2, B1, B3, B9x2. But B3 is a middle tile (3 of dots). So that's still a middle tile. So maybe not all terminals and honors.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a different pattern. For example, if I can form two more pungs. For example, two B9s. If I can get a third B9, that would be a pung. Then I have two pungs (J2 and F2), plus the B9 pung, and then the remaining tiles would need to form a pair. But with the remaining tiles, after B9 pung, I have T1, T8, T9, B1, B3. That's five tiles. To make a pair, maybe T9 and B9. But if I already have a B9 pung, then the remaining B9 would be part of the pair. Wait, no. If I have two B9s in hand and one in the pung, that's three, but since I only have two in hand, I can't form a pung unless I draw another. So that's not possible yet.\n\nAnother possibility: forming a chow with 